\begin{abstract}
Quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes can substantially reduce the qubit overhead of fault-tolerant quantum computation, but any useful architecture based on such codes also requires an efficient mechanism to implement non-Clifford gates in a fault-tolerant manner. We introduce a space-time-efficient magic state factory for high-rate qLDPC codes by combining the transversal non-Clifford gates of recently developed \textit{tricycle} codes with single-shot four-dimensional balanced product \textit{quadcycle} codes. The key ingredient is an efficient ``escape'' mechanism that teleports postselected logical magic states from tricycle codes to larger distance quadcycle codes. Under a standard circuit-level noise model at physical error rate $10^{-3}$, our factories produce \cczstate states at logical error rates as low as $10^{-9}$ with 10--40 times lower space-time cost than state-of-the-art magic state generation methods. The resulting states are directly encoded in high-rate blocks suitable for computation. We identify the quadcycle codes by an AI-assisted search and show that they can serve as excellent memories capable of single-shot error correction. Finally, we present a concrete implementation of the entire factory with highly efficient atom transport on reconfigurable neutral-atom quantum computers. Together, these results provide an end-to-end, practical protocol for producing and utilizing high-fidelity magic states within a high-rate qLDPC architecture, with the lowest space-time cost to date.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\outline{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Quantum error correction is essential for scalable quantum computation, protecting information from decoherence and from the errors introduced by noisy operations. The paradigmatic approach pairs the surface code \cite{fowler_surface_2012} with efficient Clifford logic, realized either transversally \cite{zhou2025resource} or by lattice surgery \cite{horsman_surface_2012, litinski_game_2019, gidney2025factor}. The required non-Clifford operations are performed by utilizing logical resource states, prepared through procedures such as magic state distillation \cite{bravyi2005universal, bravyi2012magic, sales2025experimental}. Such procedures require substantial overhead in both space, through the number of physical qubits used, and time, through multiple syndrome-extraction rounds or repeated attempts in repeat-until-success schemes. Recently, much effort has been devoted to reducing these costs. One of the most promising approaches involves magic state \textit{cultivation}, which hierarchically suppresses errors on an initially noisy magic state \cite{sahay2026fold, gidney_magic_2024, vaknin2026cultivation, claes2025cultivating, chen2025efficient}. Despite these efforts, scaling surface code architectures to thousands of logical qubits and the error rates that large-scale algorithms demand remains resource intensive, and magic state production continues to dominate the overhead.

High-rate quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes offer a complementary route to fault tolerance. By encoding many logical qubits in a single block while maintaining distances that grow favorably with block size, they reach low logical error rates with substantially fewer physical qubits than comparable surface code memories. Advances in circuit-level simulation and decoding have identified finite-size qLDPC codes with high encoding rates and thresholds, and low logical error rates under experimentally relevant noise levels~\cite{gidney_stim_2021, roffe2005decoding, xu2024constant, panteleev2021degenerate, bravyi_high-threshold_2024, kasai_breaking_2026, steffan2025tile}. The nonlocal checks required for realization of these codes can be implemented naturally on platforms with reconfigurable connectivity such as neutral-atom arrays \cite{xu2024constant, cain_shors_2026, menssen2026strategic, menon_magic_2025, zhao2026towards}.

The use of these codes in complete architectures is limited by two critical elements. First, while recently proposed Pauli-product measurements and code-surgery protocols allow for efficient realization of
 Clifford logic operations on qLDPC codes \cite{cohen2022low, williamson2026low, cowtan_parallel_2026, cross_improved_2025, zheng_high-rate_2025, xu2025fast, baspin2025fast, cowtan_fast_2025, zhou2026parallel, maskara2026efficient},   no native, high-throughput source of non-Clifford states is currently known that preserves the rate advantage of the surrounding code. Second, the operations on qLDPC codes typically suffer from additional temporal overhead as
 conventional protocols repeat noisy stabilizer measurements a number of times growing with the code distance. This is in contrast to fully transversal architectures~\cite{zhou_low-overhead_2025} or single-shot error correction that extracts sufficient redundancy from a constant number of rounds \cite{bombin2015single}. Single-shot behavior is known for higher-dimensional topological and expander-code constructions \cite{brown2016fault, breuckmann2016local, leverrier2022quantum}, but high-rate codes combining it with strong finite-size performance have remained elusive.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering

    \caption{\textbf{Transversal dimension-jump magic state factory.}
(a) Three tricycle blocks are prepared in $\ket{\overline{+}}$ and acted on by a transversal $\overline{\mathrm{CCZ}}$ circuit using a fan-out gadget, producing the nine-qubit hypergraph state $\detstate$ shown on the right. The gadget pairs each block with an ancilla patch of the same code through one transversal layer of CNOTs, splitting the two CCZ layers across the two patches so that every qubit takes part in a single gate. A depth-1 homomorphic CNOT teleports the magic state into three paired quadcycle blocks prepared in $\ket{\overline{0}}$. Transversal $X$ measurements of the tricycle blocks provide the teleportation outcomes and detector information used for postselection. The dashed box marks the pair used in the simulations. (b) Physical geometry of the homomorphic CNOT. The three qubit sectors of the tricycle code are aligned with their embedded counterparts in the quadcycle code, so matched qubits are coupled by a single layer of site-local CNOT gates; the remaining quadcycle qubits are idle. (c) Post-selected logical error rate per output logical qubit for the simulated code pairs, plotted against the tricycle code distance $d_{\textnormal{3D}}$, with the code parameters given below the axis. Reset, measurement, idle and two-qubit gates undergo depolarizing noise at $p=10^{-3}$. The CCZ channel is the single-qubit marginal of a three-qubit depolarizing channel of strength $p_{\mathrm{CCZ}}$. The top axis gives the acceptance fraction of a \emph{single} tricycle--quadcycle pair, for $p_{\mathrm{CCZ}}=0.1\%$ and $0.2\%$ respectively. The factory requires all three pairs to be accepted, so its yield is this value cubed. Error bars are binomial, and the dashed line is a log-linear guide through the $0.2\%$ series.}
    \label{fig:dimjump}
\end{figure*}

In this Article, we construct an end-to-end space-time-efficient magic state factory using single-shot qLDPC codes. Our approach makes use of three-dimensional balanced-product \emph{tricycle} codes that support transversal non-Clifford gates and generate several logical magic states in parallel and in constant depth \cite{menon_magic_2025, jacob2025singleshot}. However, their strongly asymmetric distances limit the fidelity with which those states can be produced and held. We pair these tricycle codes with four-dimensional balanced-product \emph{quadcycle} codes, which share algebraic structure with tricycle codes, enabling an efficient state-teleportation mechanism between them. The shared structure enables a depth-1 homomorphic CNOT that ``escapes'' the magic into the quadcycle block, which supplies larger, balanced distances and single-shot preparation and error correction in both Pauli bases.
Thanks to these single-shot properties, this entire mechanism can be realized in constant depth and is natively compatible with postselection, further refining the magic state. 
With an AI-assisted search, we identify finite-size quadcycle codes with favorable rates, thresholds, and logical error rates under circuit-level noise.

Our factories produce entangled magic states \cite{rossi_quantum_2013} across nine logical qubits, corresponding to a gate that implements the determinant of a $3\times3$ matrix over $\F$, denoted $\detstate$. We demonstrate that with homomorphic measurement \cite{huang_homomorphic_2023} gadgets, these states can be converted into two usable $\ket{\overline{\mathrm{CCZ}}}$ states. We further construct low-overhead protocols for post-processing and injection into qLDPC computation codes, as well as the required Clifford feed-forward operations. Finally, we provide depth-optimal syndrome-extraction circuits and an end-to-end compilation based on the reconfigurable neutral atom array architecture, including explicit planar layouts and atom rearrangements required from initialization through output. Under a standard circuit-level noise model at $p=10^{-3}$, the resulting factories deliver \cczstate{} states at roughly an order of magnitude lower space-time cost than the state-of-the-art magic state cultivation protocols and at a lower logical error rate, already encoded in a high-rate block ready for downstream computation.

\outline{Single-shot magic state factory}\label{sec:single-shot}

Our factory separates magic state generation from storage and processing, assigning each task to a code optimized for it. Tricycle codes support transversal non-Clifford gates and can therefore generate multiple logical magic states in parallel, but their strongly asymmetric distances limit the fidelity with which these states can be retained, typically requiring full postselection on stabilizer measurement outcomes to achieve low logical error rates. Quadcycle codes, on the other hand, provide larger, more balanced distances, together with single-shot state preparation and error correction capabilities for both $X$ and $Z$ basis stabilizers, but they lack transversal non-Clifford gates. Fortunately, these two families can be paired in such a way as to harness their complementary benefits. We demonstrate that every tricycle code can be embedded into a corresponding quadcycle code, enabling a depth-$1$ transversal homomorphic $\mathrm{CNOT}$ between the two \cite{li_transversal_2025} that we use to teleport magic from the tricycle codes to the quadcycle codes.

This teleportation mechanism enjoys two distinct advantages. First, measurement outcomes from the transversal teleportation circuit provide the necessary information for efficient and accurate postselection, suppressing the infidelity of the encoded magic state by several additional orders of magnitude. Second, the postselected magic state is now natively stored in the quadcycle code, which has large enough distance to maintain the fidelity of the magic state in subsequent rounds of error correction. The quadcycle is also better suited for downstream processing.

We consider a class of tricycle codes that support a depth-$2$ code-space-preserving $\mathrm{CCZ}$ circuit whose logical action produces the $\detstate$ hypergraph magic state. To improve the fault-tolerance properties of the $\mathrm{CCZ}$ circuit, we use a fan-out gadget on top of the tricycle code blocks, which effectively amounts to concatenation with a distance $2$ bit-type ($Z$-basis) repetition code (see \cref{fig:dimjump}). The fan-out gadget converts the $\mathrm{CCZ}$ circuit of the tricycle codes into a strictly transversal depth $1$ circuit, which preserves the circuit distance of the tricycle code and leads to significantly greater error suppression. This comes at the cost of doubling the data register, though not the peak qubit count, since the ancilla patch reuses the qubits that served as $Z$-syndrome ancillae -- see Section~\ref{app:concat} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI} for details.

The protocol, summarized in Fig.~\ref{fig:dimjump}, proceeds in three stages. First, three tricycle blocks are prepared in $\ket{\overline{+}}$, each with its fan-out gadget applied, and acted on by a depth-$1$ transversal $\mathrm{CCZ}$ circuit producing the logical hypergraph state $\detstate$. In parallel, three paired quadcycle blocks are prepared in $\ket{\overline{0}}$. A depth-1 transversal CNOT then teleports the logical state into the quadcycle blocks, after which transversal measurements of the tricycle blocks provide both the teleportation outcomes and the syndrome information used for postselection. The resulting logical $\detstate$ state can be processed to yield two disjoint $\cczstate$ states for further computation.

