The receiving code in our architecture must satisfy several requirements simultaneously. It must share sufficient algebraic structure with the tricycle code to support a transversal dimension jump~\cite{li_transversal_2025}, encode many logical qubits so that the factory remains high rate, support fault-tolerant state preparation, allow decoding using information from only a constant number of error-correction rounds, and have sufficiently large and balanced distances to preserve the fidelity obtained through postselection.

To meet these requirements, we study a particular family of Abelian multi-cyclic multivariate codes \cite{mian2026multivariate, lin2025abelian}, which for this work we refer to as \emph{quadcycle codes}. They can be understood as a four-dimensional generalization of bicycle (2D) and tricycle (3D) codes \cite{bravyi_high-threshold_2024, viszlai2024matchinggeneralizedbicyclecodesneutral, menon_magic_2025, jacob2025singleshot}, and they belong to the broad categories of balanced product \cite{breuckmann2021balanced} and lifted product codes \cite{panteleev2021quantum}. A quadcycle code is specified by four commuting sparse circulant matrices $A,B,C,D$ acting on a finite abelian group of size $n_G$. It contains $6n_G$ physical qubits arranged in six blocks, together with $4n_G$ stabilizer generators of each Pauli type. The first three factors, $A,B,C$, coincide with those defining a paired tricycle code, while $D$ introduces an additional cyclic direction. This shared structure embeds the tricycle complex within the quadcycle complex and enables the depth-1 homomorphic CNOT used for the escape operation. The complete parity-check matrices and mapping cone construction are given in Methods.

The quadcycle checks are redundant in both Pauli bases. In each basis, $n_G$ explicit relations among the stabilizer outcomes define \emph{metachecks}, which provide consistency conditions on a noisy measured syndrome. This bidirectional syndrome redundancy allows measurement faults to be identified without temporal repetition and supports single-shot preparation and error correction in both the $X$ and $Z$ bases. Consequently, the receiving blocks can be fault-tolerantly initialized and error corrected using only a constant number of syndrome-extraction rounds.

Quadcycle codes also inherit useful symmetries from their group-algebra construction. Group translations act as depth-1 code automorphisms, while an $X$--$Z$ duality supplies transversal Clifford operations and ensures equal $X$ and $Z$ distances (Methods). The finite-size codes identified in our search combine these properties with high encoding rates and substantially larger, more balanced distances than their paired tricycle codes.

The two code families therefore play complementary roles. The shared $A,B,C$ factors enable all logical magic states produced by the tricycle code to be transferred transversally, while the larger distance and single-shot structure of the quadcycle code in both the $X$ and $Z$ bases allow the accepted states to be verified, stored, and processed without sacrificing their fidelity.

Finally, we confirm that the escape codes are sufficiently good quantum memories to hold the magic states they receive. Figure~\ref{fig:4dsims} reports the circuit-level logical error rate per cycle of representative quadcycle codes under single-shot, sliding-window most-likely-error decoding. At the physical error rate $p=10^{-3}$ used in our factory simulations, this per-cycle rate is comparable to or below the logical error rate of the states the factory delivers (Fig.~\ref{fig:dimjump}c). This addresses the natural concern for downstream use: once a low-error magic state has been teleported into the quadcycle code, the code must have enough error-correction tolerance to \emph{maintain} that low error rate through subsequent storage and processing, rather than letting it grow. The comparable per-cycle rate shows that it does---the quadcycle code holds the factory-established fidelity across the additional error-correction cycles---and, because these codes are single-shot in both bases, each cycle of error correction costs only a constant number of syndrome-extraction rounds, independent of the code distance. In Section~\ref{sec:search} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI}, we additionally present several quadcycle codes which are not constrained to have a homomorphism to specific tricycle codes, found by an AI-assisted code search to have high rates and relative distances, strong single-shot circuit-level performance, and structured logical operators, demonstrating that these codes are good candidates for single-shot state-preparable LDPC quantum memories in their own right -- in particular we find quadcycle codes with parameters that improve upon those in Refs. \cite{mian2026multivariate, lin2025abelian}.

