\label{fig:4dsims}
\end{figure}

\outline{Constant-depth preparation and postselection}

We now detail the three stages of the protocol and the mechanisms that make them fault tolerant. For the code pairs considered here, each tricycle block has parameters $[[n_{\textnormal{3D}},3, (d_{\textnormal{3D}}^X,d_{\textnormal{3D}}^Z)]]$ with $d_{\textnormal{3D}}^Z\ll d_{\textnormal{3D}}^X$, and is prepared in $\ket{\overline{+}}$ alongside the ancilla patch of the fan-out gadget in $\ket{\overline{0}}$. A single transversal layer of CNOTs between them fixes the gadget's checks deterministically, requiring no additional syndrome extraction or feed-forward. All $Z$ stabilizers are fixed to $+1$ by one round of $Z$-stabilizer extraction, which is fault tolerant because the tricycle code is single-shot state-preparable in the $Z$ basis. A depth-1 transversal physical CCZ circuit then implements the logical non-Clifford operation that produces the nine-qubit hypergraph state $\detstate$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:dimjump}a). It is the fan-out gadget that reduces this circuit from depth two to depth one and thereby preserves the circuit-level distance of the factory (Section~\ref{app:concat} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI}). Meanwhile, in parallel, three paired quadcycle blocks are prepared in $\ket{\overline{0}}$ using one round of $X$-stabilizer extraction. For the pairs studied here, each quadcycle block has the same number of physical qubits as a tricycle block together with its gadget ancilla patch, twice its logical dimension, and approximately balanced distances comparable to $d_{\textnormal{3D}}^X$.

The dimension jump teleportation is implemented by a single transversal layer of physical
CNOT gates whose controls act on qubits of the tricycle code, and the targets act on part of the paired quadcycle block. The exact physical circuit that implements this homomorphic CNOT is described by the \textit{chain map} that identifies the tricycle code with a subset of the quadcycle code. Logically, this introduces a logical CNOT between all $k_{\textnormal{3D}}$ tricycle logical qubits and one of the two
$k_{\textnormal{3D}}$-dimensional sectors of the quadcycle logical qubits (Fig.~\ref{fig:dimjump}b and Methods). We confirm the logical action of this map covers all of the logical qubits of the tricycle code. This ensures every logical magic state generated in
the tricycle block is extracted and teleported to the quadcycle code.

After the dimension jump, the tricycle blocks are measured transversally in
the $X$ basis. We reject any run with a nontrivial detector outcome, while
for accepted runs the decoded logical measurement outcomes determine
Pauli-frame $\overline{Z}$ corrections on the quadcycle blocks. This
postselection substantially increases the effective $Z$ fault distance of
the protocol. A distance-$d$ code can correct at most
$\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor$ faults, whereas full error detection rejects every
nontrivial fault pattern of weight below $d$. Conditioning on acceptance
therefore approximately doubles the minimum number of faults required for a
logical failure to survive undetected. We confirm this scaling directly. Rescaling every noise rate by a common factor gives a fitted error exponent of $n_{\mathrm{eff}}=3.11\pm0.04$ for the $d_{\textnormal{3D}}=3$ factory and $3.91\pm0.07$ for $d_{\textnormal{3D}}=4$, matching the code distance rather than the $\sim d/2$ expected of a decoder without error detection (Section~\ref{app:concat} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI}).

Full error detection remains practical here because the postselected space-time region is relatively small: the tricycle blocks are modest in size and participate in only a shallow, constant-depth circuit before being measured and discarded. The resulting rejection probability is therefore manageable despite postselecting on all detector outcomes. In our protocol, the resulting suppression compensates for the strongly asymmetric distance of the tricycle code and brings the effective $Z$-error suppression closer to that set by its larger $X$ distance and by the distance of the quadcycle block.

We simulate each tricycle--quadcycle pair under circuit-level depolarizing noise at $p=10^{-3}$, with an effective channel for each physical CCZ gate of strength $p_{\mathrm{CCZ}}=p$ and $2p$. Results for the four pairs are shown in \cref{fig:dimjump}(c).
We note that since this approach uses only a small number of physical CCZ gates, their increased error rates do not significantly affect the resulting logical-level performance (Methods).

Finally, we note that the same shared algebraic structure between the tricycle and quadcycle codes enables a direct code-growth protocol where the tricycle code is grown into a quadcycle code by measuring additional stabilizers, without requiring an additional code block. Although direct growth may use fewer qubits, we find that the 3D--4D state-teleportation scheme presented here has lower space-time cost and leads to slightly better logical error rates of the produced magic states -- see Section~\ref{sec:code_growth} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI} for details on the direct code-growth procedure.

\outline{Comparison to state-of-the-art magic state preparation schemes}
We compare the magic state preparation protocol outlined in the preceding section to other state-of-the-art approaches (Table \ref{tab:comparison}). We focus on fold-transversal surface code cultivation, which uses nonlocal connectivity \cite{sahay2026fold}, and the original color code cultivation approach, which imposes more stringent connectivity constraints \cite{gidney_magic_2024}. The logical error rates and associated overheads we tabulate for the cultivation-based protocols are based on Ref. \cite{mehta_practical_2026}, which studies magic cultivation \cite{gidney_magic_2024, sahay2026fold} with mid-circuit postselection using only information that is accessible to a decoder in practice. This treatment places cultivation on equal footing with our simulations in Fig. \ref{fig:dimjump}(c), where we only use information from the measurements involved in logical teleportation, and not the transversal readout of the quadcycle code blocks, to inform our postselection. Including information from the transversal measurement of the quadcycle code, which is analogous to the inclusion of readout information in past simulations of magic state cultivation \cite{gidney_magic_2024, sahay2026fold}, leads to  orders of magnitude lower logical error rates than we observe here \cite{li_transversal_2025, menon_magic_2025, xiao2026efficient}.  

Since cultivation produces $\ket{\bar T}$ states, whereas our factory natively produces $\detstate$, we normalize the resource accounting--in terms of both the logical error rate and the expected resource cost---of these protocols by their CCZ yield, i.e., the number of $\cczstate$ states that can be extracted from the resulting magic state with additional Clifford operations. This is motivated by the fact that most primitives for quantum algorithms of interest are naturally compiled to $\mathrm{CCZ}$ gates, such as arithmetic operations for cryptographic applications \cite{gidney2025factor, zhou2025resource} or multiplexed rotations in quantum simulation applications \cite{gidney2018halving, kivlichan2020improved}. Our normalization reflects that at least $4$ $\ket{\bar T}$ states are necessary to synthesize a CCZ gate \cite{t_to_ccz}, whereas $2$ \cczstate states can be obtained from the \detstate state. More details on the space-time cost accounting resulting in the numbers in \cref{tab:comparison} can be found in Methods.

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering

    \caption{\textbf{Complete factory pipeline with downstream fault-tolerant gadgets.} Only one of the three parallel sets of code blocks is shown. The escape stage abstracts Fig.~\ref{fig:dimjump}, in which the magic state is teleported to three quadcycle blocks and postselected (not shown). Using ancillary quadcycle codes and homomorphic CNOTs, we then perform a transversal measurement that effectively addresses a single logical qubit in the $Z$ basis. As the ancillary quadcycle code is also single-shot state preparable, this measurement can be performed in constant depth. Then, depending on the logical outcome, we modify the factory to transversally prepare a resource state for the required addressable CZ corrections (Methods). Retries of the escape stage are not shown. Finally, the correction resource state is consumed transversally through gate teleportation, removing the undesired CZ gates dressing the disjoint \cczstate states. The resulting magic state has two disjoint \cczstate states and is ready to be consumed, e.g., via surgery with another code. }
    \label{fig:gadgets}
\end{figure}

Note that cultivation natively offers a mechanism to tune the overall logical error rate of the protocol at the expense of the expected resource overhead, by adjusting the retry strategy. We choose the operating point where magic state cultivation with mid-circuit postselection \cite{mehta_practical_2026} results in logical error rates equal to that of our factories at various sizes in order to offer a direct comparison.

\outline{Consuming magic downstream}\label{sec:computation}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering

\caption{\label{fig:compilation}\textbf{Qubit placement and routing for the neutral-atom implementation.}
\textbf{(a)}~Toric layout of a quadcycle code. Qubits are arranged into
    unit cells containing six data qubits, four $X$-check ancillae, and four
    $Z$-check ancillae. The corresponding tricycle cell, shown below, is
    identified with the lower row of the quadcycle cell. This shared layout
    allows the two codes to use the same routing patterns for their common
    stabilizer terms and aligns their data qubits for the homomorphic CNOT.
    The dashes inside the unit cell display which sectors are coupled for an $A$ term.
    \textbf{(b)}~Parallel atom rearrangement implementing the cyclic
    translation associated with the monomial $xy$. The translations
    are divided among at most four AODs so that all required two-qubit
    interactions can be realized in parallel. An interactive animation of the parallel atom rearrangement is included in the ancillary files with this manuscript.
    \textbf{(c)}~Routing for one layer of the transversal
    $\overline{\mathrm{CCZ}}$. Three tricycle blocks are stacked vertically,
    and atoms from the upper and lower blocks are moved into interaction range
    of the middle block. The three upper and three lower sector pairings are
    specified by six monomial translations (two are drawn), which are executed sequentially
    within each CCZ layer.}
\end{figure*}

Efficient magic state generation must be accompanied by a pipeline for consuming these states with low overhead. While a broader qLDPC architecture supporting general Clifford logic can accomplish this task \cite{webster2026pinnacle, cain_shors_2026}, we show that in our case it can be done efficiently with minimal further assumptions, in three phases: post-processing, Clifford fix-ups, and injection. Explicit constant-depth gadgets for each are given in Methods and in Sections~\ref{sec:Extracting CCZs}, \ref{sec:zero_out}, and~\ref{supp:teleporting-cz} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI} and are summarized in \cref{fig:gadgets}.

The hypergraph state \detstate is not itself a directly useful primitive for most algorithms. Measuring one logical qubit of each quadcycle block in the $Z$ basis instead yields two disjoint \cczstate states, which is optimal \cite{menon_magic_2025}, potentially dressed by a constant number of $\overline{\mathrm{CZ}}$ gates depending on the logical measurement outcomes. In Section~\ref{sec:zero_out} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI}, we show that these selective logical measurements can be performed with an efficient, constant-depth homomorphic measurement \cite{huang_homomorphic_2023} protocol using an auxiliary quadcycle code, exploiting the fact that half the quadcycle code remains in $\ket{\bar 0}$ and can serve as logical ancillae. The output is two disjoint \cczstate states dressed by additional $\overline{\mathrm{CZ}}$ gates, which can be fixed in parallel with feed-forward corrections -- in Section~\ref{supp:teleporting-cz} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI} we show that any such $\overline{\mathrm{CZ}}$ correction pattern can be implemented by efficiently preparing and consuming resource states transversally using an ancillary tricycle code. The cost of these logical measurements is not included in the space-time cost accounting in \cref{tab:comparison}, as more optimized schemes using high-rate and single-shot lattice surgery techniques may be possible, which we do not study here. Similarly, we do not include the costs of converting $\ket{T}$ states to $\cczstate$ states for the cultivation protocols in \cref{tab:comparison}.

Injection into another quadcycle block, or into a complementary code designed for Clifford logic \cite{webster2026pinnacle, cain_shors_2026}, proceeds by qLDPC code surgery, and our escape codes are well suited to it. The qubits holding the magic state admit disjoint logical $Z$ operators, which simplifies parallel surgery \cite{cohen2022low, cowtan_parallel_2026, zhang_2025_time, xu2025batched, zheng_high-rate_2025, gu_qgpu_2026}, and these operators can be measured fault-tolerantly---independently or in parallel---by gadgets with minimal qubit overhead that are highly structured and efficient to implement with neutral-atom reconfigurability. The resulting gadgets bridge naturally with those of the target code \cite{swaroop_universal_2025, cross_improved_2025}. Injection requires $d$ rounds of error correction \cite{chamberland2022temporal}, which recent single-shot surgery techniques may reduce further \cite{baspin2025fast, cowtan_fast_2025}.

Finally, consuming the magic state leaves an adaptively determined pattern of logical $\overline{CZ}$ corrections. These Clifford corrections could be delegated to the broader architecture \cite{chamberland2022temporal, cowtan_parallel_2026}. Alternatively, the factory itself can be modified to transversally produce a resource state for whichever correction is required, which is then teleported in the same way (Methods). This ensures that feed-forward corrections do not bottleneck the factory, without requiring additional Clifford-logic capabilities. In practice, however, one might use logical Pauli-product measurement gadgets constructed by generalized lattice surgery techniques to perform this correction efficiently \cite{cohen2022low, zheng_high-rate_2025, baspin2025fast, maskara2026efficient}.

\outline{Efficient implementation on neutral atom arrays}\label{sec:implementation}

We next consider the practical implementation of the proposed magic state factories, focusing on reconfigurable neutral-atom arrays.
The factory compiles into natural atom-rearrangement patterns by making use of three key features of the tricycle--quadcycle construction. Their common product structure gives a regular toric layout in which every monomial of the defining polynomials acts as a uniform cyclic translation of a whole sector, executed as parallel row and column moves (Fig.~\ref{fig:compilation}a,b). Because a matched pair shares the factors $A$, $B$, and $C$, the tricycle cell is identified with the lower row of the quadcycle cell, so the two codes share routing schedules for their common stabilizer terms and the homomorphic CNOT reduces to one layer of site-matched gates between overlaid sectors. The transversal $\overline{\mathrm{CCZ}}$ uses the same primitive: the three blocks are stacked vertically and paired by monomial translations, with the fan-out gadget allowing every gate to be applied in a single Rydberg pulse (Fig.~\ref{fig:compilation}c). Every operation in the factory therefore admits an explicit routing schedule whose duration is a constant number of array traversal times, at optimal circuit depth for both syndrome extraction and the transversal CCZ~\cite{tan26syndrome}. The explicit constructions are provided in the Methods section.

\outline{Discussion and Outlook}\label{sec:outlook}

We have introduced a qLDPC-native architecture in which transversal gates on tricycle codes generate magic in parallel and a depth-1 homomorphic CNOT escapes it into single-shot quadcycle codes, delivering \cczstate{} states at roughly an order of magnitude lower space-time cost than magic state cultivation.

One immediate improvement would be to replace full error detection with sliding-scale
postselection \cite{lee2025efficient, xie_simple_2026, wills_forced_2026}. In the current analysis, every run with a nontrivial detector outcome is rejected, which
suppresses errors strongly but reduces the acceptance fraction and therefore increases the expected space-time cost. Accepting a carefully chosen subset of nontrivial syndromes could
raise acceptance substantially at little cost in postselected fidelity. Quantifying this tradeoff for the tricycle--quadcycle pairs studied here may lead to further reductions in factory overhead. This could be complemented by simulations of our factories that exactly include the effect of the CCZ gates, which can be realized efficiently with recent methods \cite{xiao2026efficient, cercelescu2026polynomial}.

The short depth of the factory is also advantageous for neutral-atom implementations. Because each atom is initialized, used, and measured over a small number of circuit layers, there is limited time for atom loss or leakage to accumulate before the affected block is read out~\cite{baranes_leveraging_2026, bluvstein_architectural_2025}. If the final measurements can distinguish atom loss from ordinary computational-basis outcomes, the locations of lost atoms can be supplied to the decoder as additional information, further improving error-correction performance. Our numerical results use a most-likely error decoder, which provides a strong benchmark for the achievable logical performance but is too computationally expensive for real-time operation at scale. Recent machine learning decoders have achieved near-optimal performance with practical inference times for high-rate qLDPC codes \cite{blue_machine_2026, gu2026scalable}. Hierarchical decoders have likewise demonstrated similar performance with sufficient throughput~\cite{zhao2026towards, bhardwaj2026high, senior2026scalable, gu2026scalable}. These developments suggest that much of the performance reported here may be attainable with practical decoders trained directly on the factory circuits, potentially incorporating loss-resolving readout as an additional decoder input.

While the tricycle codes used in the factories presented in this work all have $k=3$ logical qubits, higher-rate variants of tricycle codes have been introduced in Ref. \cite{menon_magic_2025}, potentially leading to higher $\cczstate$ throughput when paired with appropriately matched quadcycle codes. However, the entangled hypergraph magic states produced have more complex structure than the $\detstate$ state, and extracting as many $\cczstate$ states as possible is a computationally challenging task. Moreover, the transversal $\mathrm{CCZ}$ circuits supported by these higher-rate tricycle codes are deeper. A thorough analysis of the fault-tolerance and resource costs of factories using higher-rate tricycle codes is an interesting direction for future work.

Finally, an important direction is to evaluate the factory within complete fault-tolerant computations rather than only through resource-state-level metrics. End-to-end estimates could use the logical $\ket{\overline{\mathrm{CCZ}}}$ states produced here as resources for large-scale algorithms, including Shor's algorithm \cite{shor_1994_algorithms} and simulations of the Fermi--Hubbard model \cite{hubbard_electron_1963, gutzwiller_1963_effect}. Such studies would include the production, storage, consumption, and scheduling of magic states together with the Clifford portion of the computation, and would determine how the reduced factory overhead translates into the total qubit count and runtime of practically relevant algorithms.

