\section*{Methods}

\noindent\textbf{Code constructions and properties}\\ 

We use notation and terminology as in previous works that study quantum group-algebra codes of various types -- see Refs. \cite{lin2024quantum, bravyi_high-threshold_2024, menon_magic_2025, lin2025abelian, jacob2025singleshot, breuckmann_cups_2025}.

Quadcycle codes are CSS codes constructed from a finite abelian group
$G$ of order $n_G \coloneqq |G|$ and its binary group algebra
$R=\FG$. An element $a=\sum_{g\in G}a_g g\in R$, with $a_g\in\F$,
may be identified with its support, namely the subset of $G$ on which
$a_g=1$. Multiplication in $R$ is given by group convolution. If
$G=\mathbb{Z}_{l_1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{Z}_{l_r}$, then, after
associating one formal variable with each cyclic factor,
$R\cong\F[x_1,\ldots,x_r]/(x_1^{l_1}-1,\ldots,x_r^{l_r}-1)$.
Elements of $R$ can therefore be represented as multivariate cyclic
polynomials.

For $a\in R$, let $\mathbb{B}(a)$ denote the $n_G\times n_G$ binary
matrix representing left multiplication by $a$ in the regular
representation of $G$. These matrices satisfy
$\mathbb{B}(a)\mathbb{B}(b)=\mathbb{B}(ab)$ and
$\mathbb{B}(a)^T=\mathbb{B}(a^*)$, where
$a^*\coloneqq\sum_{g\in G}a_g g^{-1}$ is the involution induced by
group inversion. Because $G$ is abelian, $R$ is commutative, and all
matrices of the form $\mathbb{B}(a)$ commute pairwise.

A quadcycle code is specified by four elements $a,b,c,d\in R$. It has $n = 6|G|$ physical qubits, grouped into six blocks of size $|G|$, together with $4|G|$ $X$-checks and $4|G|$ $Z$-checks. Their parity-check matrices are the block matrices
\begin{align}\label{eq: quadcycle checks}
    \mathcal{H}_X = \mathbb{B}\!\begin{pmatrix}
        c^* & 0   & b^* & d^* & 0   & 0   \\
        0   & c^* & a^* & 0   & d^* & 0   \\
        a^* & b^* & 0   & 0   & 0   & d^* \\
        0   & 0   & 0   & a^* & b^* & c^*
    \end{pmatrix}
    \\
        \mathcal{H}_Z = \mathbb{B}\!\begin{pmatrix}
        b & a & c & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
        d & 0 & 0 & c & 0 & a \\
        0 & d & 0 & 0 & c & b \\
        0 & 0 & d & b & a & 0
    \end{pmatrix}
\end{align}
where $\mathbb{B}$ is applied entrywise, each entry becoming its $|G| \times |G|$ circulant block and each $0$ a zero block. Each row is one stabilizer generator. Since $R$ is commutative, $\mathcal{H}_X \mathcal{H}_Z^T = 0$ over $\F$: the contributions to every entry of the product cancel in pairs, so the $X$- and $Z$-checks commute and define a valid CSS code. Every generator has weight $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ minus the weight of one element, i.e. the sum of three of $|a|, |b|, |c|, |d|$; for the weight-$(2,2,2)$ choices of $(a, b, c)$ and weight-$4$ choice of $d$ used here, the checks have weights $6$ and $8$. Each of the six qubit blocks --- the six columns of \eqref{eq: quadcycle checks} --- couples through two of the four factors in $\mathcal{H}_X$ and through the complementary two in $\mathcal{H}_Z$, which indexes the blocks by the two-element subsets of $\{a,b,c,d\}$. Labeling each by its $\mathcal{H}_Z$ pair, in column order they are $\{b,d\}$, $\{a,d\}$, $\{c,d\}$, $\{b,c\}$, $\{a,c\}$, $\{a,b\}$.

We
write
$A\coloneqq\mathbb{B}(a)$,
$B\coloneqq\mathbb{B}(b)$,
$C\coloneqq\mathbb{B}(c)$, and
$D\coloneqq\mathbb{B}(d)$. Both matrices have dimensions $4n_G\times6n_G$. After the blocks are
expanded, each binary row specifies one stabilizer generator.

\paragraph{Metachecks and the four-dimensional chain complex.}
The listed stabilizer generators are redundant in both Pauli bases.
The corresponding metacheck matrices, in circulant-expanded form, are
\begin{equation}\label{eq:quadcycle-metachecks}
    \mathcal{M}_X
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        A^T & B^T & C^T & D^T
    \end{bmatrix},
    \qquad
    \mathcal{M}_Z
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        D & B & A & C
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Each metacheck matrix has dimensions $n_G\times4n_G$ and supplies
$n_G$ explicit, though not necessarily independent, relations among
the listed stabilizers. Direct block multiplication gives
$\mathcal{M}_X\mathcal{H}_X=0$ and
$\mathcal{M}_Z\mathcal{H}_Z=0$. Together with the CSS orthogonality
condition, these maps form the five-term chain complex
\begin{equation}\label{eq:quadcycle-complex}
    \F^{n_G}
    \xrightarrow{\ \mathcal{M}_Z^T\ }
    \F^{4n_G}
    \xrightarrow{\ \mathcal{H}_Z^T\ }
    \F^{6n_G}
    \xrightarrow{\ \mathcal{H}_X\ }
    \F^{4n_G}
    \xrightarrow{\ \mathcal{M}_X\ }
    \F^{n_G}.
\end{equation}
Consecutive maps compose to zero:
$\mathcal{H}_X\mathcal{H}_Z^T=0$,
$\mathcal{M}_X\mathcal{H}_X=0$, and
$\mathcal{H}_Z^T\mathcal{M}_Z^T
=(\mathcal{M}_Z\mathcal{H}_Z)^T=0$.
Reading outward from the center of
\cref{eq:quadcycle-complex}, the physical qubits occupy
$\F^{6n_G}$; the adjacent copies of $\F^{4n_G}$ index the
$Z$- and $X$-checks; and the outer copies of $\F^{n_G}$ index the
$Z$- and $X$-metachecks.

For an ideal measured $X$-syndrome $s_X\in\F^{4n_G}$, physical
consistency requires $\mathcal{M}_X s_X=0$. Similarly, an ideal
$Z$-syndrome $s_Z\in\F^{4n_G}$ satisfies
$\mathcal{M}_Z s_Z=0$. If the measured $X$-syndrome is
$\widetilde{s}_X=s_X+\eta_X$, where $\eta_X$ is a
syndrome-measurement error, its metasyndrome is
$\mathcal{M}_X\widetilde{s}_X=\mathcal{M}_X\eta_X$. An analogous
relation holds in the $Z$ basis. The metachecks therefore provide
spatial consistency conditions that can reveal and help decode
measurement faults using a single syndrome-extraction round. Metachecks, when combined with soundness \cite{campbell2019theory}, suffice for single-shot state preparation of a code block: the ability to fault-tolerantly learn the eigenvalues of the stabilizers with only a single round of syndrome measurement. While the metachecks provide some protection against measurement errors, some low weight measurement errors will still remain undetected by the metachecks. Soundness ensures that these can effectively be treated as low weight errors acting on the data qubits. Ref. \cite{menon_magic_2025} details a proof that several of the quadcycle codes we consider here possess soundness in both bases.

This two-sided redundancy is the algebraic structure exploited by our
single-shot state-preparation and error-correction procedures in both
Pauli bases~\cite{bombin2015single, brown2016fault}. The existence of
metachecks alone, however, does not establish useful single-shot fault
tolerance: the resulting suppression also depends on the metacheck
distances, syndrome-extraction circuits, and decoder. We assess these
properties for the finite-size codes used here through the
fault-distance analysis and circuit-level simulations described
below.

\paragraph{Relation to tricycle codes.}

The elements $a,b,c$ are precisely the three factors defining the
paired tricycle code, while $d$ introduces the fourth cyclic
direction. At the level of chain complexes, the quadcycle code can be
constructed as the \textit{mapping cone} of the chain map induced by
multiplication by $d$ on the tricycle complex -- see Section~\ref{app:tcpairs} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI}. Equivalently, it is the
asymmetric balanced product of the tricycle complex with the
two-term chain complex
$R\xrightarrow{\cdot d}R$.

As detailed in Section~\ref{app:tcpairs} of the Supplementary Information~\cite{SI}, there is a canonical embedding of the
tricycle code complex into the quadcycle code complex. At the physical level,
the corresponding transversal homomorphic $\mathrm{CNOT}$ used for the dimension
jump acts on three of the six quadcycle qubit sectors. It can be shown that
$k_{\textnormal{4D}}\leq2k_{\textnormal{3D}}$. All tricycle--quadcycle pairs
used in this work saturate this upper bound,
$k_{\textnormal{4D}}=2k_{\textnormal{3D}}$. The mapping cone diagram, the
induced action on homology, and the logical map implemented by the
dimension jump are detailed in Section~\ref{app:tcpairs} of the Supplementary Information \cite{SI}.

