\section{Introduction}

Superconducting platforms offer scalable routes towards fault-tolerant quantum computing. They admit short error-correcting cycle times resulting from fast operations~\cite{google24}, high-yield device fabrication and advanced characterization techniques~\cite{van_damme_advanced_2024,lang2025characterization,Lang_CMOS_2025}, long coherence times~\cite{bland_millisecond_2025,tuokkola_methods_2025,jiang_advancements_2025}, high-fidelity two-qubit gates~\cite{marxer99fidelity2026,jiang_advancements_2025}, and leakage mitigation strategies~\cite{PRL_leakage2025,mcewen2021,McEwen2023,miao_overcoming_2023,Lacroix2025}. Key primitives for fault-tolerant quantum computing have been demonstrated~\cite{google24,google_magic2025,Ye_magicstateprep_2023,Gupta2024,wang2026super,drouet2026defects,harper2025characterising,lacroix2024scalinglogiccolorcode,caune_demonstrating_2024,besedin_lattice_2026,bence2024, lange2026}, and theoretical work has outlined pathways to achieving utility with superconducting quantum devices~\cite{Gidney2021,gidney2025factor2048bitrsa,yoder2025tourgrossmodularquantum}.

To operate quantum computers at scale, we must perform high-fidelity error-correction and fault-tolerant logic across large sections of devices. Superconducting platforms pose several challenges in this regard. First, one must design a code and architecture that can be operated in a scalable fashion given a fixed connectivity. Second, we must accurately decode errors, requiring us to obtain good noise-characterization data and measurement information in a timely and efficient manner. Third, any approach must allow for some number of defective or underperforming components. Without this, superconducting devices may face prohibitively low manufacturing yields, or the codes' performance will suffer when operating on large-scale chips~\cite{drouet2026defects}. 
Finally, scaling up to larger codes introduces more points at which qubits may leak out of the computational subspace, which can dramatically deteriorate performance~\cite{Brown_cross_brown_2020}.

In this work we demonstrate the operation of key elements of error-correction and logic across the entirety of a 156-qubit superconducting quantum computing device with a heavy-hex connectivity, \texttt{ibm\_boston}, finding substantial performance gains by using noise-informed approaches. We benchmark our error-correction performance through quantum memory experiments, in which logical qubits in a variety of stabilizer states are prepared, and then measured after several rounds of syndrome extraction. Memory experiments help determine the ability of our error-correcting scheme to protect quantum information while it is idling in a processor. We then benchmark fault-tolerant logic performance through stability experiments~\cite{Gidney2022dual,harper2025characterising,zen2026low}, which characterize the ability to perform logical measurements and manipulate codes, critical for operations such as lattice surgery~\cite{Horsman2012,fowler2012b,Litinski2019gameofsurfacecodes,fowler2019lowoverheadquantumcomputation} and logical Clifford gates~\cite{Litinski2019gameofsurfacecodes,Geher_2024,Gidney_2024_ybasis}. 
Finally, we perform lattice surgery experiments directly~\cite{Bombin_2009, fowler2012b, Horsman2012,Gupta2024,RyanAnderson2024,lacroix2024scalinglogiccolorcode,besedin_lattice_2026, Raussendorf2007, Brown2017, Litinski2019gameofsurfacecodes, Cohen2022,Thomsen2022,Davydova2025,bodeker2026} to measure the performance of fault-tolerant logic in the device. These are sensitive to logical errors in both memory and stability. 
These logical characterization experiments help determine the capability of the approaches and devices for fault-tolerant quantum computing.

Our code of choice is the dynamic compass code~\cite{lee2026scalable, zen2026low}. It is designed for the heavy-hex qubit array~\cite{Chamberland2020topological} and has a relatively low footprint for a given code distance, in comparison to other codes one might consider on this qubit geometry~\cite{McEwen2023,Benito_2025,vezvaee2025surfacecodescalingheavyhex,Hastings2021dynamically,Gidney2022,Kesselring2024,Davydova2023floquet,Setiawan2025}. The dynamic compass code also demonstrates a threshold in both memory and stability~\cite{zen2026low}, meaning theoretically that it can be scaled to improve performance in memory and logic. 

We demonstrate the benefits of noise-informed decoding in memory experiments operated over the entire device, as well as the utility of this approach in decoding stability and lattice surgery experiments. We use averaged circuit eigenvalue sampling (ACES)~\cite{flammia2021b,hockings2024,hockings2025improvingerrorsuppressionnoiseaware} to extract circuit-specific Pauli noise models that inform decoding. 
We can obtain more information by extracting data from measurement pulses beyond the binary outcomes, and using this data for soft decoding
~\cite{pattison2021improvedquantumerrorcorrection,Ali2024,Majaniemi_2026}. We show that soft information and ACES noise data interact in stability performance in ways not previously observed. In particular, soft information improves the decoding of stability experiments only when ACES information is not used, bringing performance with a decoder informed by a simpler noise model on par with ACES-informed decoding.

As larger superconducting chips are manufactured, a degree of heterogeneity in the performance of components such as couplers and qubits, and even completely defective components, will need to be tolerated in order to maintain acceptable yields from the manufacturing processes. The quantum processor \texttt{ibm\_boston}, for example, typically displays very low median error rates, but has a small number of qubits or couplers suffering from noise $1$ -- $2$ orders of magnitude higher~\cite{ibm_compute}. We therefore seek quantum error correction strategies that can maintain high performance despite this heterogeneity. 
There has been considerable recent effort towards designing defect-exclusion strategies for the surface code~\cite{Stace2009thresholds, Auger2017, Strikis2021, Siegel2023adaptivesurfacecode, Lin2024codesign, Wei2025low, Leroux2025snakes, Debroy2025luciinsurfacecode, mishmash2025excising,  wolanski2026automated, wei2026adaptivedeformationcolorcode,higgott2025handlingfabricationdefectshexgrid,anker2025optimizedmeasurementschedulessurface} followed by some demonstrations in experiment~\cite{drouet2026defects,kim2026luciibmhardwareerror}. However, comparatively little work has been devoted to similar strategies for codes on layouts with more general connectivities, and in particular, lattices that are less connected than a square grid, making defect avoidance more challenging~\cite{McLauchlan2024accommodating,higgott2025handlingfabricationdefectshexgrid,wolanski2026automated}. Here, we design and implement an approach for defect-mitigation in the dynamic compass code operated on the heavy-hex qubit array. Our strategies improve performance substantially in both memory and stability in \texttt{ibm\_boston}, beyond what is achievable with noise-informed decoding alone. 



Measurement data also provides information about when leakage has occurred~\cite{lee2026scalable,drouet2026defects,sundaresan2022,Krinner2022}. We can obtain estimates for the role of leakage in limiting performance by post-selecting on runs in which it is likely that no qubits have leaked. For our lattice surgery results, we introduce a technique for leakage \textit{pre}-selection~\cite{barron2026observableestimationabsenceclassical}, in which we do not execute runs when the starting state is likely to have already experienced some leakage. We also investigate the effect of pre- and post-selection more broadly. We find substantial improvements from removing shots that prepared an initial code state with error, and also from using logical gap post-selection~\cite{Log_gap_2024,gidney_yoked_2025,meister2024efficientsoftoutputdecoderssurface,Smith2024}. Finally, we observe that post-selecting on the convergence of a belief propagation stage of the decoder~\cite{higgott2023improved} results in similar improvements in a different experiment.

\subsection{Summary of Results}
We here present a summary of our results, presented in detail in \cref{sec:Results}.
We introduce defect exclusion techniques for the dynamic compass code in Section~\ref{sec:dynamic_compass_code}. When combining  ACES-informed BeliefMatching decoding, defect mitigation and leakage post-selection, in full-device memory experiments, we observe $4.11\%$ and $2.56\%$ logical error probabilities per round in the $Z$ and $X$ bases, respectively. 
These are $32.3\%$ and $35.2\%$ improvements compared to baseline performance, in the two bases, respectively (Section~\ref{sec:memory_results}). These results were qualitatively consistent across multiple days of data collection. Our best results, without any post-selection, found $4.4\%$ and $2.9\%$ logical error per round in the two bases, respectively. On a different day in which the noisy components were performing worse, we observed improvements resulting just from defect exclusion of up to $40\%$, depending on the decoder used. We observe that defect exclusion still produces sizeable gains even with good noise characterization. 
We find that our $7\times 7$ memory patch with defect-mitigation meets or surpasses the average $5\times 5$ memory patch performance in both bases, indicating a key role for defect exclusion strategies in pushing monolithic devices into the sub-threshold regime. 

In stability, we observe continual error-suppression up to 30 rounds of syndrome extraction only when we exclude defects, whereas we find plateaus in patches without defect mitigation starting around 12 rounds. At 30 rounds, we find an overall improvement of $56\%$ in failure probability from our techniques (Section~\ref{sec:stability_results}). We observe that soft information improves performance only when using reported (not ACES) noise for decoding.

When performing lattice surgery, we obtain lower bounds on Bell fidelities of $77.3\%$ when just using pre-selection filters. When combining this with logical gap post-selection, we obtain a Bell fidelity above $97.6\%$ with a post-selection yield of $27\%$ (Section~\ref{sec:lattice_surgery}). In the Appendices, we report further results for memory, stability and lattice surgery (including logical state teleportation) experiments. 

