\subsection{Dynamic Compass Code for a Heavy-Hex Qubit Array Excluding Defective Components}\label{sec:dynamic_compass_code}

We perform experiments using the IBM Quantum \textit{Heron} class of superconducting quantum processor, where 156 qubits are arranged on a plane with connectivity described by a heavy-hex lattice, displayed in \cref{fig:Main_code_patch}.
We present results on the device \texttt{ibm\_boston}, which supports mid-circuit measurements and high fidelity (median $\simeq 99.7\%$) two-qubit gates. The fidelity of processor components vary day-to-day, but underperforming components tend to cluster around one valence-2 qubit at site $46$, and one valence-3 qubit at site $85$ (see Sec.~\ref{sec:defect_exclusion_details} for discussion of defective qubits and couplers in the device).

We run 
experiments with the dynamic compass code~\cite{zen2026low,lee2026scalable}, and test strategies to treat the defective components. This code is compatible with the heavy-hex qubit array and demonstrates a threshold in both logical bases~\cite{zen2026low}. The code is a dynamic, or Floquet, code with $X$-checks ($Z$-checks) consisting of four-body (two-body) measurements on data qubits. The code is designed from the gauge measurements of the heavy-hex code~\cite{Chamberland2020topological,harper2025characterising} for use on the heavy-hex layout. We consider a $7\times 7$ code for the memory experiment as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Main_code_patch}, and a $5\times 5$ code for the stability experiment as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_patch}.
The $7\times 7$ memory patch is the largest $n\times n$ patch that fits on the device. Both patches are large enough that they cannot avoid operating across regions with defective components.

Our sequence for the $7\times 7$ code with defect exclusion is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Main_code_patch}(b). Importantly, the excluded qubits (black dots) and their neighboring couplers are never used. Instead, we redesign gauge measurements around the defective qubits to form superstabilizers that are not supported on the defective components. This results in some $X$ gauge checks being reduced to weight-$2$ or $3$, and some $Z$-gauge checks reduced to weight-$1$.

Additionally, the dynamic nature of the code allows for all stabilizers to remain low weight.
The dynamic compass code~\cite{zen2026low, lee2026scalable} does not maintain a fixed stabilizer group; we measure a sequence of anti-commuting checks that changes the instantaneous stabilizer group over a finite period~\cite{Hastings2021dynamically,alam2025baconshorboardgames}.
The four-step periodic sequence of check measurements is shown in  Fig.~\ref{fig:Main_code_patch}(b).  In Steps 1 and 3 we measure all $X$-checks in the code. In Step 2 we measure a subset of the $Z$-checks and in Step 4 we measure the remainder of the $Z$-checks. 
The partition of $Z$-checks into Steps 2 and 4 allows for $X$ stabilizers in the instantaneous stabilizer group of each step to remain low weight.  
Away from defects, they have a maximum weight of 8 on data qubits, while near defects this may increase up to 10.

The four-step sequence can be parallelized to only require two measurement layers~\cite{harper2025characterising}. Since $Z$ and $X$ check measurements occur on disjoint subsets of measure qubits, we can measure $X$ and $Z$ checks simultaneously. Therefore, we delay the measurement of $X$ measure qubits in Step 1 (Step 3) until the $Z$ measure qubits are ready to be measured in Step 2 (Step 4). 
Aligning the measurement layers reduces the overall time taken for syndrome extraction as well as data qubit idling, since measurements consume the majority of wall-clock time of a syndrome extraction cycle. 
See Section~\ref{sec:syndrome_mmt} for further details on the syndrome measurement circuits around defects.

\subsection{Memory Experiments}\label{sec:memory_results}

\begin{figure}
    \centering

    \caption{\textbf{Defect exclusion and decoding strategies.} We compare excluding no defects, excluding qubit 85, excluding qubit 46, or excluding both (this patch is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Main_code_patch}). We also compare three different decoders: PyMatching with a simple uniform noise model, BeliefMatching with a reported noise model, and BeliefMatching with an ACES-informed noise model. Even with better noise information and more accurate decoders, the gains of defect exclusion remain pronounced. We plot the percentage reduction in logical error per round compared to no defect-mitigation for the three defect exclusion choices and three decoders. Data was collected on 2026/05/02.\label{fig:dropout_vs_decoder}}
\end{figure}

Using the $7 \times 7$ dynamic compass code defined in the previous section, we perform memory experiments to characterize its performance. 
In these experiments, we initialize the code in a logical stabilizer state (either $\ket{0}$, $\ket{1}$, $\ket{+}$ or $\ket{-}$), perform a number of rounds of syndrome extraction, counted by the number of layers of measurements in the circuit
after initialization, and then measure the data qubits to determine the state of the logical qubit fault-tolerantly. We average over the two basis state preparations ($\ket{0}$ and $\ket{1}$, or $\ket{+}$ and $\ket{-}$) for each memory experiment ($Z$ or $X$).

Figure~\ref{fig:decoder_defect_improvements}(a) compares the logical error per round using the four noise informed decoding strategies presented in Table~\ref{tab:noise_models}.
In all cases, we use a BeliefMatching decoder~\cite{higgott2023improved} informed by noise characterization data. In the first case (reported noise), we use a Pauli noise model based on the coupler and qubit error information provided by IBM, specific to the day of the experiment (see Section~\ref{app:reported_noise}). In the second case (ACES noise), we generate noise models based on ACES noise characterization experiments run contemporaneous with the corresponding memory experiments. In some cases, ACES noise information obtained from a different day or that was specific to a slightly different syndrome extraction circuit needed to be used due to absence of more accurate data. 
The ACES noise model captures more of the device inhomogeneity than the reported noise, incorporating, for example, circuit-layer-specific differences in gate noise profiles resulting from other gates that are operated simultaneously. The more accurate noise model results in modest improvements over the reported noise data. 

Excluding underperforming components around qubits 85 and 46 resulted in further improvements. This defect exclusion consistently resulted in a reduced logical error per round, on every day of data collection. The combination of ACES information and defect exclusion improved the logical error per round by $23.2\%$ and $ 18.0\%$ for the $Z$ and $X$ basis memory experiments, respectively, compared to reported noise decoding.

We see continued improvements in logical error per round when recalibrating measurements using the IQ data and removing shots that may have experienced leakage through post-selection. In the $Z$ basis, we find an overall improvement in logical error of $32.3\%$, and in the $X$ basis we find an improvement of $35.2\%$. 

The IQ plane data is obtained from measurements, which carry more than just binary classification information. 
To perform leakage post-selection, we first prepare and measure all qubits in states $\ket{0}$, $\ket{1}$ and $\ket{2}$, and fit the resulting IQ data spreads to weighted Gaussian mixture models (see Ref.~\onlinecite{lee2026scalable}). From these models we can use the IQ data from QEC experiments to determine the probability that each measurement detects a leakage event. We discard any shots from the analysis in which we find a probability of $\geq 90\%$ that the qubit has leaked. Furthermore, for the shots that are not discarded, we use the IQ data to produce probabilities $p_0$ and $p_1$ that the qubit is in state $\ket{0}$ or state $\ket{1}$, respectively, and take the measurement outcome to be $\text{argmax}_i\; (p_i)$. The use of IQ data and post-selection results in a further reduction in logical error per round of $11.8\%$ and $21.0\%$ in $Z$ and $X$ bases, respectively (see Fig.~\ref{fig:decoder_defect_improvements}).
For all data, we use a BeliefMatching decoder~\cite{higgott2023improved}. 

We note that to calculate logical error per round in Fig.~\ref{fig:decoder_defect_improvements}(a), we fit the logical error rate data, averaged over both basis states for that memory experiment, to an exponential decay $p_L = \frac{1}{2}(1-Ae^{-t/\tau})$ and extract the logical error per round from that fit, which is $\epsilon_L = \frac{1}{2}(1-e^{-1/\tau})$ where the uncertainties are propagated from the fitted decay constant $\tau$. Figure~\ref{fig:decoder_defect_improvements}(b) shows the memory survival $1 - 2 p_L$ per syndrome extraction round for both $X$ and $Z$ memory. For each data point we collect $4$ runs of $2048$ shots (runs have longer separations than shots), all on the same day, and aggregate together all runs when calculating the logical error probabilities.

We ultimately find a logical error per round of $2.56\% - 4.11\%$ when operating this full-device memory experiment (performing leakage post-selection). Even without leakage post-selection, our best memory results achieve similar logical errors per round (see Appendix~\ref{app:further_mem_data}). The best logical errors per round observed for memory were $4.4\%$ and $2.9\%$ in the $Z$ and $X$ bases, respectively, without leakage post-selection (data collected on a different day than that of Fig.~\ref{fig:decoder_defect_improvements}). These data compare favorably to other experiments on heavy-hex devices. Ref.~\onlinecite{sundaresan2022} found heavy-hex code logical errors per round of $\lesssim 6\%$ for $Z$ memory experiments and $\lesssim 12\%$ for $X$. Results from our previous work~\cite{lee2026scalable,harper2025characterising} sat around $3 - 4\%$ logical error per round on carefully chosen regions of the device, running codes of size $3\times 3$ or $5\times 5$. We compare this to our current experiments run with the full monolithic device while achieving similar performance.

