\paragraph*{Comparison of defect-exclusion strategies ---}In Fig.~\ref{fig:dropout_vs_decoder}, we report the percentage improvement in logical error obtained by excluding different defect combinations (compared to a baseline of no defects excluded; note that data was collected on a different day to that of Fig.~\ref{fig:decoder_defect_improvements}). We compare excluding only the defective components around qubit 46, only those around qubit 85, and excluding both. We find that excluding both sets of defective components results in the lowest logical error per round. While we observe that q85 exclusion is no different to excluding both defects in the $Z$ basis, we observe that $X$ basis memory experiments are improved by excluding both defects.

We also observe that the benefit of excluding defects is retained even when supplying the decoder with very accurate ACES noise information. Indeed, in Fig.~\ref{fig:dropout_vs_decoder}, we compare the percentage improvement obtained by defect exclusion across three different decoder approaches, and observe little difference between using reported and ACES noise, indicating that the defects set a floor in performance that cannot be passed by improving the decoder.
However, using a worse decoder and noise model does result in the largest gains from defect exclusion, of $\sim 40\%$ and $\sim 36\%$ in $Z$ and $X$ bases respectively. These result from using a minimum-weight perfect matching decoder~\cite{higgott2021pymatching} informed using a simple global noise model that was chosen to result in reasonable performance across all days tested (See Section~\ref{app:reported_noise}). Hence, noise-informed decoding can reduce the improvement seen from defect exclusion up to a point, but beyond this, active defect exclusion results in a roughly constant percentage improvement. With an ACES-informed BeliefMatching decoder, we see up to $\sim 28\%$ and $\sim 27\%$ improvement in $Z$ and $X$ bases, respectively. We provide further data used to generate Fig.~\ref{fig:dropout_vs_decoder} in Appendix~\ref{app:further_mem_data}.

As a control, in Appendix~\ref{app:further_mem_data}, we demonstrate that excluding a non-defective component in the same patch worsens performance, indicating that the gains we observe are indeed due to defect exclusion strategies.

\paragraph*{Average performance at break-even when scaling a dynamic compass code from $5\times 5$ to $7\times 7$ ---}To gather information about the current state of \textit{Heron} class quantum processors, we interrogate how far scaled memory experiments are from break-even on \texttt{ibm\_boston}. We present results for both $Z$ and $X$ bases in \cref{fig:d5_d7_patches}. Using BeliefMatching and ACES information, we find that the $7\times 7$ patch with defect exclusion is very close to the median and mean $5\times 5$ patch performance in both bases. Note that we only consider the nine $5\times 5$ patches that lie within the footprint of the $7\times 7$ patch. Indeed, in the $X$ basis, defect exclusion allows the $7\times 7$ patch to beat the average $5\times 5$ patch logical error per round. However, we observe that the average $3\times 3$ code still outperforms both larger code sizes, owing to many small codes that can avoid all underperforming components (see Appendix~\ref{app:further_mem_data}).

