We quantify the expected performance of fault-tolerant logic gates by performing stability experiments with the dynamic compass code on \texttt{ibm\_boston}~\cite{Gidney2022dual,harper2025characterising,zen2026low}. We construct a $5\times 5$-sized stability patch that covers a large proportion of the device, see Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_patch}(a) for placement. 
We cannot fit a stability patch of the same size as the memory patch, owing to the additional row of $X$-measure qubits required, so we choose a $5\times 5$ stability patch.  

In our stability experiment, the boundary conditions are determined such that the product of all $X$-checks in a given time step is equal to the identity. Hence, in the absence of noise, the product of all $X$-check measurement outcomes at each round of the circuit is $+1$. If, after decoding over $N$ measurement rounds, this observable reads $-1$, we report a logical failure. Measurement-based gates such as lattice surgery require a product of stabilizers to be accurately measured. Stability experiments therefore help to quantify the ability of the device to perform lattice surgery operations, as the observable being tested is a product of stabilizers. The distance of a stability experiment is independent of the size of the patch. Conversely, we expect the circuit distance to scale linearly with the number of syndrome extraction rounds. Hence, when below threshold, we expect to see an exponential decrease in failure probability as we increase the number of rounds. Note that, because mid-circuit resets cannot be performed in \texttt{ibm\_boston}, the time-like distance of our experiments is $\lceil N/2\rceil$, where $N$ is the number of syndrome extraction/measurement rounds~\cite{Geher:2025aa}.

We perform stability experiments on a patch of sufficient size that, for any placement, it will necessarily include some defective components.  As such, our experiments test the effect of defect exclusion on stability experiments. We chose to position the patch as in Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_patch}(a) so that qubit 85 lay close to the middle of the patch. Defects appearing near code boundaries can only be excluded by deforming the boundaries around the defects, thereby resulting in a smaller patch, which itself results in better stability performance~\cite{Gidney2022dual}. The placement of the patch in our experiments therefore allowed us to test the effect of defect exclusion on stability, without changing the size of patch used. For this reason, even though qubit 46 was also covered by the patch, we did not exclude it. In Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_patch}(b), we show the measurement sequence for the defect-excluded stability patch.

In Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_results}, we compare stability performance across four decoding strategies similar to the $7\times 7$ memory experiments. In stability experiments, however, we also employ soft measurement information in some of our strategies; see Table~\ref{tab:noise_models}.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_results}(a), we compare the failure probability after $N=5$ measurement rounds and $N=30$ rounds for each method. 
The overall improvements from a combination of ACES-noise decoding, soft information, and defect exclusion are striking.
Firstly considering no post-selection, at $N=5$, we see an improvement in the failure probability from $0.372$ to $0.254$, giving a reduction in overall failure probability of $32\%.$  At $N=30$, we see an improvement from $0.346$ to $0.21$, giving a reduction of $39\%$. 
When combining leakage post-selection with the above, we see the largest improvements, of up to $56\%$ at $N=30$. However, leakage post-selection on the full patch was not sufficient to reproduce the effects of defect exclusion (see Appendix~\ref{app:more_stability_data}).

Crucially, the combination of defect exclusion and leakage post-selection produces clear error suppression up to $N=30$ rounds. Figure~\ref{fig:stability_results}(b) illustrates failure probability as a function of the number of rounds. Without defect exclusion, the failure probability is suppressed initially by increasing the number of syndrome extraction rounds, but plateaus after around 12 rounds. With defect mitigation excluding defective components around qubit 85, we see not only an improvement of the failure probability after a few rounds but we observe suppression up to $30$ syndrome extraction rounds. We note that Pauli noise simulations do not capture this plateau phenomenon in stability experiments, and we attribute it to leakage in the device.

The stability experiments we perform here are large when compared with other recent experiments, in terms of the number of check measurements per round (16 for the patches in Fig.~\ref{fig:stability_patch}, compared to 12 in Ref.~\cite{drouet2026defects}, 6 in Ref.~\cite{harper2025characterising}, and 4 in Ref.~\cite{caune_demonstrating_2024}). Using a $5\times 5$ patch for our stability experiment, our techniques achieve stability failure probabilities as low as $15.3\%$, which is comparable to previous results ($14.7\%$ in Ref.~\cite{harper2025characterising}, $12.6\%$ with defect-exclusion in Ref.~\cite{drouet2026defects}, $17.6\%$ in Ref.~\cite{caune_demonstrating_2024}). Indeed, all of these previous examples of stability experiments were conducted on smaller patches where we expect performance to be better.

\paragraph*{Soft information ---}We also consider the effect of soft information independently on decoder accuracy. From IQ measurement data, we obtain probabilities that each qubit was in state $0$, $1$, or leaked. When decoding with soft information, we set the prior probability of a measurement error in the decoder to be $\min(p_0, p_1)/(p_0 + p_1)$. Figure~\ref{fig:soft_info_stability} compares the effect of soft vs.\ hard decoding using ACES and reported noise models. 
When using ACES noise, we observe negligible improvement from soft information, but when using reported noise, soft information produces a statistically significant improvement, making the results indistinguishable from the ACES noise results. 
This indicates that for stability experiments, soft information from IQ-plane data may be sufficient for achieving high accuracy decoding, even when a detailed noise model is unavailable. 

\subsection{Lattice Surgery}
\label{sec:lattice_surgery}
To evaluate the performance of the device across memory and logic simultaneously, we perform lattice-surgery experiments. We use these to prepare logical Bell states and perform logical state teleportation. Through combinations of pre-selection and post-selection filters, we achieve logical Bell fidelities and teleportation success probabilities over $97\%$. We report logical Bell state preparation experiments here, and teleportation experiments in Appendix~\ref{app:teleport}.

Figure~\ref{fig:lattice_surgery_loc} shows the placement of two surgery experiments: we consider two $3\times 3$ codes (Fig.~\ref{fig:lattice_surgery_loc}(a)) and two $4\times 3$ codes (Fig.~\ref{fig:lattice_surgery_loc}(b)). This code positioning enables lattice surgery but avoids any significantly under-performing couplers or qubits.
For the $3 \times 3$ surgery experiment we use the standard heavy-hex code measurement schedule~\cite{Chamberland2020topological}, where all the Pauli-Z checks are measured simultaneously in every round of measurements. In this case, due to the dimensions of the experiment, the Pauli-$X$ instantaneous stabilizers are weight-$6$. As such, we gain little by using the dynamic compass code schedule to further decrease the weight of the stabilizers. The gains from more frequent $Z$-check measurement outweigh the cost of slightly larger-weight $X$ stabilizers. 
The full four-step sequence of the dynamic compass code is used for the $4\times 3$ lattice surgery. Owing to the greater $X$-distance of the $4\times 3$ codes, this lattice surgery experiment should offer greater protection against logical $Z$ failures in memory during the merge step.

To characterize the surgery experiments, we 
calculate lower and upper bounds of the logical Bell fidelity. 
We do this by measuring the logical Bell-state correlators $\langle\bar{Z}_1\bar{Z}_2\rangle$ and $\langle \bar{X}_1\bar{X}_2\rangle$ from logical qubit readouts after the lattice surgery. Data qubits are initialized in $\ket{0}$ and the lattice surgery is performed for $r=5$ rounds (this maintains a time-like distance of 3 for circuits without resets) to read out the $\bar{X}_1\bar{X}_2$ observable (product of blue shaded plaquettes in Fig.~\ref{fig:lattice_surgery_loc}), obtaining outcome $m_X$. Subsequently, the patches are split and one more round of syndrome extraction is performed for both separated codes. Then, data qubits are measured out either in the $Z$ basis to determine the $\bar{Z}_1\bar{Z}_2$ observable or in the $X$ basis to determine $\bar{X}_1\bar{X}_2$ again. We then perform decoding with BeliefMatching, 
with observables set to either the readout outcomes of $\bar{Z}_1\bar{Z}_2$ or the product of $m_X$ with the readout of $\bar{X}_1\bar{X}_2$.
We are unable to measure $\bar{Y}_1\bar{Y}_2$ fault-tolerantly.
However, we can estimate bounds on the Bell fidelity using $\frac{1}{2}(\langle m_X \bar{X}_1\bar{X}_2\rangle + \langle\bar{Z}_1\bar{Z}_2\rangle)\leq F_{\rm Bell} \leq \frac12(1+\min\{\langle m_X \bar{X}_1\bar{X}_2\rangle, \langle \bar{Z}_1\bar{Z}_2\rangle)$ (See Section~\ref{sec:bell_fidelity}).

