Without any filtering, the logical Bell fidelity bounds are given in the first row of~\cref{tab:lattice_surg_pre_sel}. 
In teleportation experiments, we initially observe an average success probability across all $X$ and $Z$ basis states of $79.6\%$. To improve these results we implemented slightly different strategies for the different experiments.

\paragraph*{$3\times 3$ surgery ---} We perform pre- and post-selection as follows.  First, we pre-select on shots where we have high confidence that the initial state does not exhibit any leakage, and where we were correctly projected into the initial code state (see~\cref{sec:prefilters}).  In addition, we consider logical gap post-selection using an exclusive decoder~\cite{Smith2024,English_2025,Lee2026PS,chen2026} as follows: we compute a PyMatching confidence score for each shot, given by the difference in minimum matching weight between the decoder’s preferred logical sector and the best correction constrained to the opposite logical sector. We only keep shots whose gap exceeded a chosen threshold. These post-selected shots are then decoded using a BeliefMatching decoder. 

\Cref{fig:lattice-main}(a) shows the results of applying pre-selection filters, together with the effect of using post-selection with an increasing logical-gap threshold, for the $3\times 3$ lattice surgery experiment.
The Bell fidelity bounds obtained using pre-selection are given in the second row of~\cref{tab:lattice_surg_pre_sel}. This strategy filtered out $86.7\%$ of the experiment starting attempts.
Logical gap post-selection then improved these fidelities further. For example, rejecting shots with a logical gap of less than 8 provides  improvement of the logical Bell fidelity lower/upper bound to $95.8\%-97.8\%$ with a relative yield of $34.3\%$ (the total yield after all filters is $4.55\%$). With a threshold of 9, we can achieve Bell fidelities above $97\%$, with a post-selection yield of $27\%$. For teleportation experiments, only using pre-selection filters produced an average success probability of $87.9\%$, while performing pre- and post-selection, we obtain an average success probability of $98.25\%$ with a relative yield of $45.9\%$ (see Appendix~\ref{app:teleport}).

In order to determine whether we are still in an error-correction regime, or if we are simply post-selecting on shots with no detectors firing, we calculate the average number of detector violations per-round after post-selection. At a logical gap threshold of $8$, the average per-round detector violations only drops from $1.16$ to $0.91$, indicating that we are still actively correcting many errors over all shots performed. We report further data, showing the effect of all individual pre-filters and post-selection, in Appendix~\ref{app:teleport}.

We are not aware of results that demonstrate this high a fidelity when performing lattice surgery between two error-correcting ($d>2$) codes in superconducting architectures. For example, Ref.~\onlinecite{besedin_lattice_2026} report post-selected Bell fidelities of up to $78\%$ with $6.3\%$ shot yield when performing lattice surgery between two $d=3$ repetition codes. Meanwhile, Ref.~\onlinecite{wang2026latticesurgery} reports up to $92.5\%$ with $0.5\%$ shot yield for lattice surgery between two $d=3$ surface codes.

\paragraph*{$4\times 3$ surgery ---}When using the combination of pre-selection filters on the $4\times 3$ surgery results, we obtain a yield of $10.34\%$ and a Bell fidelity lower bound slightly worse than the $3\times 3$ results under the same conditions (second row of \cref{tab:lattice_surg_pre_sel}).
When adding post-selection to this experiment, we found little benefit from using the PyMatching confidence score as a post-selection filter. This is because increasing logical gap threshold eventually decreased Bell fidelity estimates; the maximum attainable Bell fidelity lower bound was less than $91.5\%$. 
Instead, we use the BeliefMatching decoder as a confidence filter.
We only kept shots where the belief-propagation stage converged after 20 iterations, computing the logical correction on that accepted subset.
With this post-selection filter we found that neither the leakage pre-filter nor the code-state preparation filter improved the results. 
It appears that conditioning acceptance on the ability of the belief-propagation stage to converge already removes such problematic runs.
Using this filter we were able to achieve a logical Bell fidelity range of $95.8\%-97.5\%$ with a yield of $9.64\%$ and an average per-round detector activity of 1.4 (see \cref{fig:lattice-main}(b)). 

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:Discussion}

In this work, we have demonstrated key elements of quantum error correction and fault tolerance with the dynamic compass code on a heavy-hex lattice of 156 superconducting qubits, by running high-fidelity memory, stability and lattice surgery experiments. We combine noise-informed decoding, defect exclusion, and leakage post-selection to achieve significant improvements in memory and stability, and a collection of pre- and post-selection techniques to achieve large improvements in lattice surgery. 
Our results reveal key lessons for future fault-tolerant devices.

We have seen that leaky and under-performing components can severely limit error-correction performance. 
We analyze the role of leakage in our experiments by post-selecting on runs without any detected leakage. This leakage post-selection consistently improved performance of logical elements. A variety of strategies exist and have been tested for reducing leakage in error-correction experiments~\cite{Aliferis_Terhal_LRU_2007,miao_overcoming_2023,mcewen2021,McEwen2023,PRL_leakage2025,Lacroix2025,Werninghaus2021,Battistel2021,xin2025improvederrorcorrectionleakage}, and these will likely be necessary in future generations of devices.
Defects resulted in bottlenecks to improving performance in our experiments and we overcame this issue by introducing defect exclusion techniques for the dynamic compass code. Large numbers of defects will limit the code distances achievable in manufactured chips, indicating that future devices may need to improve component homogeneity in order to see further large performance gains.

We have also seen very sizable benefits from combining pre- and post-selection filters in lattice surgery, which is of significant relevance to future error-correction experiments.  
The pre-selection filters that we use in lattice surgery experiments are qualitatively different than post-selection techniques. Pre-selection allows us to probe the failure mechanism associated with the logic gate itself, removing those mechanisms associated with initializing in a leaked state of the processor or failing to prepare the code space of one or both of the codes. These filters could in principle be applied before any long experiments are begun, as part of a prepare-until-succeed loop~\cite{Gupta2024}. Hence, these could potentially be important components of future experiments for maximizing observed fidelities. 
Separately, lattice surgery often features in magic state factories, which can effectively make use of post-selection~\cite{Litinski2019gameofsurfacecodes}. Performing logical gap post-selection on lattice surgery within such parts of a fault-tolerant architecture could potentially be a scalable~\cite{English_2025,Smith2024,Log_gap_2024,gidney_yoked_2025,meister2024efficientsoftoutputdecoderssurface} method to further boosting resource state fidelities with the additional advantage of aborting low-confidence shots early. 

Finally, we observe that good noise characterization, accurate decoding and measurement soft information, while improving performance up to a degree, are ultimately insufficient to overcome the deleterious effects of defects. However, we have not investigated decoders with higher accuracy than BeliefMatching~\cite{higgott2023improved}. 
Neural network decoders~\cite{Torlai_2017,Bausch2024,Varbonov2025,senior2026scalablerealtimeneuraldecoder} or most-likely error decoders~\cite{beni2025tesseractsearchbaseddecoderquantum} could be investigated in future work to see if these could potentially overcome the need to remove certain under-performing components.
We note that the gains seen from using soft information in stability experiments disappear when using ACES-noise information. This could be practically useful for future experiments, since collecting ACES data can be costly in terms of device time and classical processing time required. However, it would be important to know in what regimes these findings hold.

Future generations of quantum processors will need to improve component homogeneity, to avoid the need for excessive defect exclusion, and will require fast, multi-level resets or other techniques to actively remove leakage. They will need to maintain high component fidelities and homogeneity while scaling to larger sizes of chip. The use of defect exclusion strategies that are far less costly in terms of distance and fidelity could be achieved by considering designs with more connectivity between qubits~\cite{drouet2026defects}. There will in general be an interplay between chip homogeneity, component fidelity, and fabrication yield, with greater qubit connectivity increasing tolerance to defects. We envisage that these future devices will allow for further key error-correction primitives to be demonstrated, such as magic state injection and distillation, a greater array of Clifford operations, and real-time accurate decoding.  Finally, future quantum processors with a large number of logical qubits will require even more qubits and/or other innovations such as long-range connectivity that will allow for larger-distance quantum codes and lower logical error rates.

