\begin{abstract}
We show that the fast surgery framework of \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc} can be instantiated using at most $\tilde{O}(n(k + d))$ ancilla qubits, improving on the previously achievable $\tilde{O}(nkd)$ overhead. This result also establishes that fast surgery can be performed at a cost comparable to the $\tilde{O}(nk)$ overhead required for regular surgery \cite{cowtan2025parallel}. We build the surgery ancilla by first starting with a dense, but expanding system; and then applying expansion-preserving sparsification methods from gauging \cite{williamson2024gauging} and code embeddings \cite{yuan2026unified, yuan2026quantumweightreductionlayer}.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Intro}

The prospect of practical quantum computers is hampered by the operational costs they are expected to incur \cite{campbell2017roads}. 
Compared to their classical counterparts, state-of-the-art quantum computers consume significant space and time resources in order to run even the simplest algorithms \cite{Litinski2019, gidney2021twenty}.
The necessity to reduce this footprint has thus shaped much of the current QEC landscape \cite{Bravyi2024,xu2024constant,gidney2025factor2048bitrsa, webster2026pinnacle, cain2026tenthousand}.

Space overheads are generally attributable to the type of codes used \cite{gottesman2014constant,cohen2022surgery, nguyen2025constantspace,bhardwaj2026highrate}. 
For a given scheme, it is conventionally written that one uses $n$ physical qubits to encode $k$ logical qubits. 
Naturally, the smaller the ratio $n/k$ is, the smaller the space overhead. 
The need for code families satisfying a constant $n/k$, for growing $k$, spurred the early developments \cite{freedman2002systolic, kim2007hurwitz, zemor2009cayley, delfosse2013tradeoffs, tillich2014hypergraph, guth2014quantum} of \qcode{QLDPC} codes \cite{breuckmann2021balanced}. 
It can be useful to note that such a quest has a natural horizon: once constant-rate families are discovered, further improvements can only reduce the space overhead by a constant factor.

Conversely, time overheads emerge from inefficiencies in the handling of the data. As codes are designed to insulate the logical information they protect from the exterior environment, the best codes will also make it harder to operate said information. This can be coarsely quantified by $d$, the number of errors a code can undergo without failing; and aside from a few particularly well behaved exceptions \cite{breuckmann2024fold, eberhardt2024fold}, performing a gate will typically require $\Omega(d)$ time \cite{Litinski2019}. 

Among existing methods to perform such logical gates, lattice surgery \cite{bombin2009measurements, horsman2012surgery} has emerged as a prominent component of low-overhead architectures due to its ability to generalise to \qcode{QLDPC} codes \cite{cohen2022surgery, williamson2024gauging, cowtan2025parallel}.
Despite surgery also suffering from a $\Omega(d)$ delay in its original formulation, recent results have shown that it can be made fast \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc, cowtan2025fast} -- i.e. performed in $O(1)$-time -- generally at the cost of an increased space footprint. While in some special cases constant space overhead can still be achieved \cite{chang2026constanttime}, the most general construction for fast surgery requires an additional $\tilde{O}(nkd)$ physical qubits \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc}.

In this work we show that fast surgery can be performed with $\tilde{O}(n(k + d))$ physical qubits, improving on $\tilde{O}(nkd)$, and bringing the cost in line with the $\tilde{O}(nk)$ overhead of regular surgery \cite{cowtan2025parallel}. Our main result is \Cref{theorem:main}, which constructs a \qcode{QLDPC} surgery ancilla system with large enough expansion and co-systole to satisfy the requirements for fast surgery in \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc}. 

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{mainthm}
\label{theorem:main}
    Let $C_\bullet$ be a \qcode{QLDPC} code, and $\measured \subset H_1(C)$ an arbitrary subspace of logicals of dimension $\kappa = \dim \measured$. Then there exists $D^{\text{sparse}}_\bullet$, $\gamma_\bullet^{\text{sparse}}: D^{\text{sparse}}_\bullet \to C_\bullet$:

    \begin{equation}
        \begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
        	{D_2^{\text{sparse}}} \& {D_1^{\text{sparse}}} \& {D_0^{\text{sparse}}} \\
        	{C_2} \& {C_1} \& {C_0}
        	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
        	\arrow["{\gamma_2^{\text{sparse}}}", from=1-1, to=2-1]
        	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
        	\arrow["{\gamma_1^{\text{sparse}}}", from=1-2, to=2-2]
        	\arrow["{\gamma_0^{\text{sparse}}}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
        	\arrow[from=2-1, to=2-2]
        	\arrow[from=2-2, to=2-3]
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation}
    with the following properties
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $D^{\text{sparse}}$ is $1$-expanding relative to $\gamma_1^{\text{sparse}}$
        \item $\im [\gamma_1^{\text{sparse}}] = \measured$
        \item $\syst^1(D^{\text{sparse}}) \geq \syst^1(C)$ 
        \item $\partial_1^{D^{\text{sparse}}}$ and $\gamma_\bullet^{\text{sparse}}$ are $O(1)$-sparse
        \item $\dim D_0^{\text{sparse}} \leq \tilde{O}(n(k-\kappa + d))$, where $n = \dim C_1$
    \end{enumerate}

\end{restatable}

The primary practical limitation of our construction is that $\partial_2^{D^{\text{sparse}}}$, corresponding to the metachecks, is not necessarily sparse. 

\section{Prelimaries}

An $m$-dimensional chain complex, $(C_\bullet, \partial_\bullet)$, over $\F_2$ is a collection of $\F_2$-vector spaces, $\left\{ C_i \right\}_{i=0}^{m}$, and linear boundary maps $\left\{ \partial_i: C_{i+1} \to C_i \right\}_{i=1}^{m}$

\begin{equation}
    C_\bullet =
    \begin{tikzcd}
        C_D \arrow[r, "\partial_D"] & C_{D-1} \arrow[r, "\partial_{D-1}"] & \cdots \arrow[r, "\partial_2"] & C_1 \arrow[r, "\partial_1"] & C_0
    \end{tikzcd}\, ,
\end{equation}

such that the boundary maps satisfy $\partial_{i-1} \circ \partial_{i} = 0$. 
We assume that each vector space $C_i$ comes with a distinguished basis and its elements are called $i$-chains.
Moreover, the elements of $B_i = \im \partial_{i+1}$ are called $i$-boundaries, and the elements of $Z_i = \ker \partial_i$ are called $i$-cycles. 
The homology groups are defined as $H_i\left(C_\bullet\right)$, i.e., they correspond to cycles that are not boundaries

\begin{equation}
    H_i\left(C_\bullet\right) = \ker \partial_i / \im \partial_{i+1}\, .
\end{equation}

We can similarly define the cohomology groups, which for chain complexes over $\F_2$-vector spaces are defined as

\begin{equation}
    H^i\left(C^{\bullet}\right) = \ker \delta_i / \im \delta_{i-1}\, ,
\end{equation}

where $\delta_i = \partial_{i+1}^T$ for the considered $\F_2$ vector spaces. 
Since the vector spaces are equipped with distinguished bases, we define a norm $\left \| \cdot \right \|$ on the $i$-chains given by the Hamming weight. 
Then the $i$-systole and $i$-cosystole are defined by

\begin{align}
    \text{Syst}_i\left(C_{\bullet}\right) &= \min \left\{ \left\| v \right\| \mid v \in Z_i \setminus B_i \right\}, \\
    \text{Syst}^i\left(C^{\bullet}\right) &= \min \left\{ \left\| v \right\| \mid v \in Z^i \setminus B^i\right\}.
\end{align}

We say a linear map $A$ is $\omega$-bounded if $\norm{Av} \leq \omega \norm{v}$ for any $v$. In a finite dimensional space this corresponds to $A$ having column weight at most $\omega$. We further say that $A$ is $\omega$-sparse if both $A$ and $A^T$ are $\omega$-bounded.

\subsection*{Maps, Cones, and Fibers}
There exists a natural notion of a morphism for chain complexes. 
Suppose we have two chain complexes, $\left(C_\bullet, \partial^C\right)$ and $\left(D_\bullet, \partial^D\right)$, along with a set of homomorphisms $\gamma_i: D_i \to C_i$. 
If the homomorphisms and the boundary maps satisfy the commutativity condition

\begin{equation}
    \gamma \partial = \partial \gamma \, ,
\end{equation}

then the map $\gamma_i$ describes a chain map between the complexes $C_\bullet$ and $D_\bullet$, which we refer to as \emph{homomorphic}.
We can represent this visually via the following commuting diagram

\begin{equation}
    \begin{tikzcd}
        \cdots \arrow[r] & D_{i+1} \arrow[r] \arrow[d, "\gamma_{i+1}"] & D_{i} \arrow[r] \arrow[d, "\gamma_{i}"] & D_{i-1} \arrow[r] \arrow[d, "\gamma_{i-1}"]& \cdots \\
        \cdots \arrow[r] & C_{i+1}  \arrow[r] & C_{i}  \arrow[r] & C_{i-1}  \arrow[r] & \cdots
    \end{tikzcd} \, .
\end{equation}

We can define a new chain complex from the above complex. 

\begin{definition}
    Let $(C_\bullet, \partial^C), (D_\bullet, \partial^D)$ be $m$-dimensional chain complexes, and $\gamma: D_\bullet \to C_\bullet$ a chain map. Then the mapping cone $\cone(\gamma)$ of $\gamma$ is defined as:
    \begin{gather}
        \cone(\gamma)_i = C_i \oplus D_{i-1} \\
        \partial^{\cone(\gamma)}_i (c, d) = (\partial_i^C c + \gamma_{i-1} d, \partial^D_{i-1} d) 
    \end{gather}
    Now, for a chain map $\tau_\bullet: C_\bullet \to D_\bullet$, we can define the mapping fiber $\fib(\tau)$ of $\tau$ as:
    \begin{gather}
        \fib(\tau)_i = D_{i+1} \oplus C_i \\
        \partial^{\fib(\tau)}_i (d,c) = (\partial^D_{i+1} d + \tau_i c, \partial^C_i c)
    \end{gather}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    The difference between $\cone(\gamma)$ and $\fib(\gamma)$ is only a matter of grading. In the first, $\cone(\gamma_i)$ is indexed in terms of the "left" component $C_i$, while $\fib(\gamma)_i$ would be indexed relative to the "right" component $D_i$.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{Systolic expansion}
There is a notion of expansion in chain complexes which generalizes the notion of edge expansion in graphs, which can be viewed as $1$-dimensional chain complexes.

\begin{definition}[Systolic expansion \cite{gromov2010singularities, kaufman2016isoperimetric, evra2016bounded}] \label{def:systolic_expansion}
    Let $D_\bullet$ be an $m$-dimensional complex and let $\epsilon > 0$. 
    For $i < m$ we say that the vector space $D_i$ is $\epsilon$-systolic expanding if

    \begin{gather}
        \forall f \in D_i, \quad \norm{\partial_i f}  \geq \epsilon \cdot  \dist(f, Z_i) \\
        \dist(f, Z_i) = \min_{z \in Z_i} \norm{f + z}
    \end{gather}
\end{definition}
A chain homomorphism from $D_\bullet$ to $C_\bullet$ naturally gives rise to a notion of \emph{relative} expansion. 
\begin{definition}[Relative expansion \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc}]
Let $D_\bullet$ be an $m$-dimensional complex, $\gamma : D_\bullet \rightarrow C_\bullet$ a chain map, and let $\epsilon > 0$. 
    For $i < m$ we say that $D$ is $\epsilon$-systolic expanding relative to $\gamma_i$ if for all $f \in D_i$:

    \begin{equation}
        \norm{\partial_i f}  \geq \epsilon  \min \left \{  \dist(\gamma_i (f), \gamma_i  (Z_i(D))), \syst_i(C) \right \}
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}

\section{Main results}

Fundamentally, constructing a viable surgery ancilla is a problem about \emph{map sparsification}. For the sake of notation, let us fix
\begin{equation}
    C_\bullet = C_2 \to C_1 \to C_0, \quad \measured \subseteq H_1(C)
\end{equation}
Where $C_\bullet$ corresponds to the code of interest, and $\measured$ corresponds to the subspace of logicals we want to measure. Then the measurement could be simply realised by picking a lift $\iota: \measured \to C_1$, and the merged code would then be described by $\cone(\iota)$ \cite{Ide2025}.
\begin{equation}
    \begin{tikzcd}
	& {\measured} & \\
	{C_2} & {C_1} & {C_0}
	\arrow["\iota", from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow[from=2-1, to=2-2]
	\arrow[from=2-2, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
However, crucially, the map $\iota$ is generally non-sparse, and yields an undesirable high weight ancilla. In order to correct for this, one can then try to sparsify $\iota$; this is the approach adopted in \cite{hastings2023quantumweightreduction, williamson2024layer, williamson2024gauging}, where $\iota$ takes the interpretation of additional stabilisers added to $C_\bullet$. 
Formally \cite{yuan2026unified, yuan2026quantumweightreductionlayer}, this sparsification replaces the ancilla $\measured$ by a sparse complex $D_\bullet^{\measured}$, and a sparse chain map $\gamma^{\measured}: D_\bullet^{\measured} \to  C_\bullet$, such that the maps $[\gamma_1^{\measured}]: H_1(D^{\measured}) \to H_1(C)$, $[\iota]: \measured \to H_1(C)$ satisfy:
\begin{equation}
    \im [\gamma_1^{\measured}] = \im [\iota]
\end{equation}
And the merged cone is then described as $\cone(\gamma^{\measured})$:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{tikzcd}
	{D^{\measured}_2} & {D^{\measured}_1} & {D^{\measured}_0} \\
	{C_2} & {C_1} & {C_0}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=2-3]
	\arrow[from=2-1, to=2-2]
	\arrow[from=2-2, to=2-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

For fast surgery in particular \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc}, the ancilla $D_\bullet$ (we drop the $\measured$ superscript) is itself generally built as a mapping fiber $D_\bullet = \fib(\tau)$ of some map $\tau: C_\bullet \to E_\bullet$.

 This structure is chosen as it is particularly amenable to preserving some desirable properties from $C_\bullet$, like its co-systole. Once that requirement is satisfied, the most notable remaining difficulty in building a suitable $D_\bullet$ such that it also exhibits (relative) systolic expansion \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc}. 

 In order to resolve the problem of producing expansion, we follow an approach inverse to \cite{baspin2025fastsurgeryquantumldpc}: we begin with a (dense) ancilla system $D_\bullet^{\text{dense}}$ that immediately exhibits expansion.
\begin{equation}
    D_\bullet^{\text{dense}} = \fib\left(\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
    	{C_2} \& {C_1} \& {C_0} \\
    	\& T
    	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
    	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
    	\arrow["\sigma", from=1-2, to=2-2]
    \end{tikzcd}\right)
\end{equation}
For some $\sigma$ we describe in the following. 
 Write $\pi : Z_1 \to H_1(C)$ the projection from the cycles to the corresponding homology group. Then we define
\begin{equation}
    \lpreimage = \pi^{-1} \measured \subset Z_1(C)
\end{equation}
Writing $\kappa = \dim \measured$, in general ${\lpreimage}$ can be thought of as the span of $\{l_1, \dots, l_{\kappa}\}$, a selected basis for $\measured$; plus $B_1(C)$. As a result we have $\dim {\lpreimage} = \kappa + r_X$, with $r_X  = \dim B_1(C)$. The space ${\lpreimage}$ should be understood as the target image of $\gamma_1 \ker \partial_1^D$. 

From here on we write $q = k - \kappa$ for readability. Note that $\measured^\perp \subset H^1(C)$ has exactly dimension $q$. Then we can pick $\{l^\star_{1}, l^\star_{2}, \dots, l^\star_{q}\}$ a basis of co-cycles corresponding to $\measured^\perp$ as a natural choice for $\sigma_{\text{cocycles}}$ is as follows:

\begin{equation}
    \sigma_{\text{cocycles}} = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        l^\star_{1} \\
        l^\star_{2} \\
        \vdots \\
        l^\star_{q}
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
Indeed $\partial_1$ eliminates all non-cycles, while $\sigma_{\text{cocycles}}$ eliminates those cycles that are not in $\lpreimage$. And we therefore get:
\begin{equation}
    \ker \sigma_{\text{cocycles}} \cap \ker \partial_1 = L
\end{equation}
However, we are still lacking expansion. We will remediate that issue by introducing a second matrix $\sigma_{\text{boost}}$ such that:
\begin{equation}
    \norm{\sigma_{\text{boost}} u} + \norm{\sigma_{\text{cocycles}} u} +\norm{\partial_1 u} \geq d: u \not\in \lpreimage
\end{equation}

This condition is in fact much stronger than the relative expansion we need. We will then focus on the subset of chains that needs boosting
\begin{equation}
    S_d = \{ c \in \im \begin{bmatrix}
        \partial_1 & \sigma_{\text{cocycle}}
    \end{bmatrix}: 0< \norm{c} < d\}
\end{equation}
Those syndrome have to be boosted, which we will do through using random codes. From \Cref{lemma:random-coding-boosting} with $m = q + m_Z \leq O(n)$, and $M_Z = \dim C_0 \leq O(n)$ we get 
\begin{align}
    \sigma_{\text{boost}}&: C_1 \to \mathbb{F}_2^{t}, \quad t \leq O(d\log(n))\\
    \sigma_{\text{boost}} &= R\begin{bmatrix}
        H_{\text{logical}} \\
        \partial_1
    \end{bmatrix} \\
     \norm{\sigma_{\text{boost}} u} &+ \norm{\sigma_{\text{cocycles}} u} +\norm{\partial_1 u} \geq d: u \not\in \lpreimage
\end{align}

Note that the resulting ancilla satisfies a stronger version of expansion, which we call the $d$-threshold property.

\begin{definition}[$d$-threshold property]
Let $D_\bullet$ be an $m$-dimensional complex, $\gamma : D_\bullet \rightarrow C_\bullet$ a chain map. Then, for $i < m$ we say that $D$ has the $d$-threshold property relative to $\gamma_i$ if:

    \begin{align}
        \forall x \in D_i: \gamma_i(x) \not\in \gamma_i(Z_1(D)), \quad \norm{\partial_1^D x} \geq d
    \end{align}
\end{definition}

From these observations, we obtain a natural choice for $\sigma$ with the expected properties.

\begin{lemma}[Dense expanding map]
\label{lemma:expanding-pcm} 

Let $C_\bullet$ be a code, and $\measured \subset H_1(C)$ a subspace of logicals. Then there exists $\sigma : C_1 \to \F_2^s$ such that:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item There is a $1$-sparse chain map $\gamma_\bullet: \fib(\sigma) \to C_\bullet$ such that $\fib(\sigma)$ has the $\syst_1(C)$-threshold property relative to $\gamma_1$. 
    \item $\im [\gamma_1] = \measured$
    \item $\syst^1(\fib(\sigma)) \geq \syst^1(C)$
    \item $s \leq O(q+d\log(n))$
\end{enumerate}

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    The previous conversation proves most points for $\sigma$ taken as follows: 
    \begin{equation}
        \sigma = \begin{bmatrix}
            \sigma_{\text{cocycles}} \\ \sigma_{\text{boost}}
        \end{bmatrix}, \quad \sigma: \F_2^n \to \F_2^s \cong \F_2^q \oplus \F_2^t
    \end{equation}
    And we take $\gamma_\bullet$ as follows:
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
	\& {C_2} \& {C_1} \& {C_0} \\
	\&\& {\mathbb{F}_2^s } \\
	{C_2} \& {C_1} \& {C_0}
	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{\gamma_2} =  \text{id}_{C_2}"{pos=0.7}, from=1-2, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-4]
	\arrow["{\sigma}", from=1-3, to=2-3]
	\arrow["{\gamma_1} =  \text{id}_{C_1}"{pos=0.7}, from=1-3, to=3-2]
	\arrow["{\gamma_0} =  \text{id}_{C_0}"{pos=0.7}, from=1-4, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow[from=3-2, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation}

    The remaining point to prove is that of the co-systole. 
    While mapping fibers spontaneously preserve systoles, co-systoles can seem a bit trickier to preserve due to the addition of new redundancies through $\sigma^T$. 
    Fortunately, the structure of these redundancies is well controlled, as $\im \sigma^T = (\ker \sigma)^\perp = \lpreimage^\perp$, which should generally be understood as being constituted of co-boundaries, and some non-trivial co-cycles commuting with $L$. 

    We will see that the fact that $\sigma \partial_2 = 0$ (which is necessary for $\fib(\sigma)$ to be a valid chain complex) strongly restricts the systole.
    We have:
    \begin{align}
        Z^1(\fib(\sigma)) &= Z^1(C) \\
        B^1(\fib(\sigma)) &= B^1(C) + \im \sigma^T
    \end{align}
    Which, for the sake of convenience, we re-write as:
    \begin{align}
         B^1(C) + \im \sigma^T &= B^1(C) + (\ker \sigma)^\perp\\
    \end{align}
    From $\sigma \partial_2 = 0$, we have $B_1(C) \subset \ker \sigma$, and therefore $(\ker \sigma)^\perp \subset Z^1(C)$. This gives:
    \begin{align}
        \syst^1(\fib(\sigma)) &= \min_{ l \in Z^1(\fib(\sigma)) \setminus B^1(\fib(\sigma))}\norm{l }\\ 

        & = \min_{ l \in Z^1(C) \setminus B^1(C) + (\ker \sigma)^\perp}\norm{l } \\

        & \geq \min_{ l \in Z^1(C) \setminus B^1(C) }\norm{l } \\
        & = \syst^1(C)
    \end{align}
\end{proof}

However, $\sigma$ as chosen here is a dense matrix, and we are again reduced to having to sparsfiy the new stabilisers given by the rows of $\sigma$. 

This new starting point might seem disappointing as we had started with an equally dense matrix $\iota$. We highlight here that the actual contribution of that step is that sparsifying $\iota$ requires coming up with an expander complex, while here sparsifying $\sigma$ merely requires preserving the already present expansion.

We then show that the weight-reducing gauging gadgets of \cite{williamson2024gauging} are indeed expansion-preserving. We then use a dual version of those to build a similar expansion-preserving degree-reducing gadget.

\begin{remark}
    It is a question of general curiosity to understand how natural it is to base the ancilla $D_\bullet$ off a copy of $C_\bullet$. Interestingly, the co-systole preservation exhibited by \Cref{lemma:expanding-pcm} relies precisely on $D_2 = C_2$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Main theorem}

\mainthm*

\begin{proof}
    We start by $D^{\text{dense}}_\bullet = \fib(\sigma)$, given by \Cref{lemma:expanding-pcm}. We obtain the following $1$-sparse chain map $\gamma_\bullet: \fib(\sigma) \to C_\bullet$:
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{tikzcd}
        	& {C_2} & {C_1} & {C_0} \\
        	&& T \\
        	{C_2} & {C_1} & {C_0}
        	\arrow[from=1-2, to=1-3]
        	\arrow["{\gamma_2}"{pos=0.8}, from=1-2, to=3-1]
